{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts sit materially above realized prices across all examples (often by ~5–12 EUR), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent returns are negative.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model is slow to recognize regime shifts lower; it keeps forecasting sideways-to-higher while the market continues falling. Increase weight on the most recent 5–10 days’ slope; if 10-day trend is down, force the forecast drift down (not flat).",
    "Underestimates downside momentum: realized paths show extended, monotonic declines; model mean-reverts too early. Reduce mean-reversion strength when volatility is elevated and recent drawdown is large; allow longer persistence of negative drift.",
    "Horizon error grows with time: overprediction widens at longer horizons (weeks 3–4), with forecasts sometimes rising while reality keeps falling. Apply a horizon-dependent haircut (increasing with lead time) during bearish regimes.",
    "Overshoot on rebound expectations: model frequently projects rebounds/step-ups (late-horizon upticks) that do not materialize. Suppress late-horizon rebound unless there is confirmed stabilization (e.g., 3–5 consecutive non-lower closes)."
  ]
}
